<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02112</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fb8031ac-e8cf-4631-83f6-d99794187a1a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_5c4d70dc-e487-45ef-b54c-4b2011eccc9d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The expression \(9t + 7.25\) represents the cost to purchase tickets for a concert, where \(t\) is the number of tickets. A group of friends paid $79.25 for tickets. How many tickets were bought?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>6 tickets</para><para>8 tickets</para><para>10 tickets</para><para>12 tickets</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.2.1.</para><para>The correct answer is 8 tickets.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="fbee7c79-87a0-445a-a892-cc8b898f7e73" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 2 – 4.</para><para><quote>A model rocket is launched from a launchpad. The function modeling the height of the rocket, in feet, \(t\) seconds after being launched is shown.</quote></para><para>\(f(t) = -15t^2 + 58t + 8\)</para>
      <para>Which equation would you solve to find the time at which the rocket hits the ground?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(-15t^2 + 58t + 8 = 0\)</para><para>\(-15t^2 + 58t = 8\)</para><para>\(-15t^2 + 58t = 0\)</para><para>\(15t^2 + 58t + 8 = 0\)</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 8.2.2 and 8.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(-15t^2 + 58t + 8 = 0\).
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="af0f6d88-afdc-49c8-8cdb-39521ee2e593" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Use a non-graphing method to find the solution to the previous equation to determine the time that has passed when the rocket hits the ground.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>2 seconds</para><para>3 seconds</para><para>4 seconds</para><para>5 seconds</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 8.2.2 and 8.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 4 seconds.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_77177ead-21bf-47d5-8c05-3f3b97d87f3e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>How high off the ground is the launchpad from which the rocket lifts off?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>0 feet</para><para>2 feet</para><para>8 feet</para><para>15 feet</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 8.2.2 and 8.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 8 feet.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_0750a278-9b95-4e76-9997-19b7297b90d3" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 5 – 8.</para><para><quote>The expressions \(t(90 − 3t)\) and \(-3t^2 + 90t\) define the same function. The function models the number of lumens emitted by a spotlight in an art exhibit over the course of \(t\) seconds.</quote></para><para>Which equation represents the time when the spotlight is not emitting any light?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(t(90 − 3t) = -3t^2 + 90t\)</para><para>\(-3t^2 + 90t = 675\)</para><para>\(-3t^2 + 90t = 0\)</para><para>\(-3t^2 + 90t = 30\)</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 8.2.2 and 8.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(-3t^2 + 90t = 0\).
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="e5fc91a9-34b4-455f-b981-0837a3479e43" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>At what time(s) does the spotlight not emit any light?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>0 and 30 seconds</para><para>10 and 25 seconds</para><para>Only 0 seconds</para><para>15 seconds</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 0 and 30 seconds.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_3ce1ef69-1b98-4969-acd5-c48e61d1d1f3" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The artist planned for the spotlight to emit 600 lumens at 10 seconds from the start. At what other time will the spotlight emit 600 lumens of light?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>0 seconds</para><para>20 seconds</para><para>25 seconds</para><para>30 seconds</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 20 seconds.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_34dd878a-a1d9-490a-bcfe-161061de6d55" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>At 5 seconds, the spotlight will emit 375 lumens. Find another time that the spotlight emits 375 lumens.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>0 seconds</para><para>10 seconds</para><para>15 seconds</para><para>25 seconds</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 25 seconds.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="d1aa4d5f-093f-4b11-90d5-d40945962b23" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>9-10.</para><para><quote>A bowling alley models the revenue from sales in one day as a function of the cost to rent a lane per hour, \(c\). Here are two expressions defining the same revenue function.</quote><quote>\(c(160 − 10c)\)</quote><quote>\(160c − 10c^2\)</quote></para><para>According to this model, how high would the cost to rent a lane have to be for the bowling alley to make $0 in revenue?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>$8</para><para>$10</para><para>$14</para><para>$16</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is $16.
     </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_71966d95-aaa8-45f2-8ea8-abb97354839d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What equation can you write to determine the lane cost that would allow the bowling alley to make $640 in revenue?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\( c(160 − 10c) = 0\)</para><para>\( c(160 − 10c) = 640\)</para><para>\( 160c − 10c^2 + 640 = 0\)</para><para>\( 160c − 10c^2 = 0\)</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(c(160 − 10c) = 640\).</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
